skill: "cohort-retention-curves"
sections[3]:
  - "# cohort-retention-curves\n\nBuild cohort retention curves, fit the decay, and name the segments that fall under benchmark. Use when monthly retention moves, before a pricing or packaging change, or when product asks which acquisition cohort actually sticks. Produces the evidence base that health scores and PRD problem statements are built on.\n\nOwner: role-churn-analyst (pod-customer, customer_success). Direct human technical prose; zero conversational fluff.\n\n## Inputs and Outputs\n\nContract in TOON, as consumed by the runbook:\n\n```\nallowed_tools[4]:\n  - read_file\n  - write_file\n  - run_command\n  - ask_question\ninputs: \"activity event export with account id, signup date and activity dates; plan and segment attributes plan-map.toon; benchmark table retention-benchmarks.toon\"\noutputs: \"agents/artifacts/customer/retention-cohorts.toon with cohort grid, retention per period, fitted decay per segment, red_flag_segments[]; handoff to product and health scoring\"\n```\n\nThe agent refuses to run with missing or stale inputs: it stops and emits an ask_question naming the missing file instead of inventing values.\n"
  - "## Workflow\n\n1. Define the cohort key and the activity definition first (what counts as active); without a written definition the curves are untestable and the run stops here.\n2. Export events and derive the cohort grid: signup period (weekly or monthly by volume) crossed with plan and acquisition segment.\n3. Compute retention per period per cohort with run_command; use the same activity window length for every cohort so curves are comparable.\n4. Smooth nothing below the display layer; publish raw curves and mark small cohorts (below the minimum size in benchmarks) as low-confidence instead of hiding them.\n5. Fit a decay curve per segment; report the fitted half-life and compare it against retention-benchmarks.toon.\n6. Flag red_flag_segments[]: any segment whose period-N retention falls under benchmark by more than the tolerance, with the evidence row attached.\n7. Check the survivorship trap: exclude accounts whose churn is involuntary (payment failure) from the voluntary curve and note them separately for the dunning loop.\n8. Write retention-cohorts.toon; hand the red-flag segments to role-product-manager for PRD problem statements and to customer-health-score as the calibration set.\n"
  - "## Handoff\n\nsegments to role-product-manager; calibration set to customer-health-score.\n"
